---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../../styles/doc.css';
import HeroEmblem from '../../components/HeroEmblem.astro';
---
<Base title="Bitcoin Hash Playground: SHA-256, Base58Check, Bech32 - OpenBitcoin" description="Run the hashes and encodings Bitcoin uses in your browser: SHA-256, double SHA-256, RIPEMD-160, HASH160, Base58Check and Bech32. Nothing is sent.">
<main class="wrap doc-wrap">
  <div class="doc-head">
    <div class="doc-kicker"><a href="/tools">Tools</a></div>
    <h1>Hash and encoding playground</h1>
    <HeroEmblem name="fingerprint" />
    <p class="doc-lede">Run the primitives bitcoin is built from on your own bytes: SHA-256, double SHA-256, RIPEMD-160, HASH160, Base58Check, Bech32 and Bech32m, plus a byte order flip. All of it in this tab.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="doc-grid">
  <div>
    <div class="hx-fail" id="hx-fail" hidden role="alert">
      <b>Self-test failed</b>
      <span id="hx-failwhy">One of the checks this page runs on load did not match its known answer, so every card has been switched off rather than show a digest that might be wrong.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="hx-card">
      <div class="hx-top">
        <div class="hx-lab">Shared input</div>
        <div class="hx-modes" role="group" aria-label="How to read the shared input">
          <button type="button" class="hx-m is-on" data-m="text">Text, UTF-8</button>
          <button type="button" class="hx-m" data-m="hex">Hex</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <textarea id="hx-in" class="hx-ta" rows="2" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" placeholder="abc" aria-label="Shared input bytes"></textarea>
      <div class="hx-bar">
        <div class="hx-ex" aria-label="Example inputs">
          <span>Try:</span>
          <button type="button" data-ex="abc">abc</button>
          <button type="button" data-ex="empty">empty</button>
          <button type="button" data-ex="pubkey">public key</button>
          <button type="button" data-ex="merkle">merkle root</button>
        </div>
        <div class="hx-cnt" id="hx-cnt">3 bytes</div>
      </div>
      <div class="hx-seed" id="hx-seed" hidden role="alert">
        <b>That looks like a recovery phrase.</b> It was cleared and nothing was hashed. A seed phrase belongs on paper or metal, never in a browser tab, and no honest website has any use for one. If those words really are not a seed, switch to hex and paste the bytes instead.
      </div>
      <p class="hx-fine">Never type a seed phrase, a private key, an xprv or a 2FA secret into any website, this one included. Hashing a secret inside a browser tab does not make it safe.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="hx-priv">Every function below runs on your machine. This page makes no network requests with what you type, so the bytes never leave the tab. Each card starts out following the shared input; type in a card to give it its own.</p>

    <div class="hx-cards">
      <div class="hx-c">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>SHA-256</b><em>32 bytes out</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">One pass of SHA-256. The workhorse behind mining, merkle trees and message commitments.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i" id="hx-i-sha" rows="1" data-fn="sha" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" aria-label="Input for SHA-256"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar"><span class="hx-fol" id="hx-fol-sha">following shared input</span><button type="button" class="hx-re" data-fn="sha" hidden>follow shared</button><span class="hx-n" id="hx-n-sha"></span></div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-sha"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>Double SHA-256</b><em>32 bytes out</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">SHA-256 applied twice. This is the hash bitcoin means when it says hash: txids, block ids, merkle nodes, checksums.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i" id="hx-i-dsha" rows="1" data-fn="dsha" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" aria-label="Input for double SHA-256"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar"><span class="hx-fol" id="hx-fol-dsha">following shared input</span><button type="button" class="hx-re" data-fn="dsha" hidden>follow shared</button><span class="hx-n" id="hx-n-dsha"></span></div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-dsha"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>RIPEMD-160</b><em>20 bytes out</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">A 1996 European hash, kept in bitcoin for one job: shortening public keys. Implemented inline, since no browser ships it.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i" id="hx-i-rmd" rows="1" data-fn="rmd" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" aria-label="Input for RIPEMD-160"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar"><span class="hx-fol" id="hx-fol-rmd">following shared input</span><button type="button" class="hx-re" data-fn="rmd" hidden>follow shared</button><span class="hx-n" id="hx-n-rmd"></span></div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-rmd"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>HASH160</b><em>20 bytes out</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">RIPEMD-160 of SHA-256. Feed it a public key and you get the 20 bytes that sit inside an address or a witness program.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i" id="hx-i-h160" rows="1" data-fn="h160" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" aria-label="Input for HASH160"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar"><span class="hx-fol" id="hx-fol-h160">following shared input</span><button type="button" class="hx-re" data-fn="h160" hidden>follow shared</button><span class="hx-n" id="hx-n-h160"></span></div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-h160"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c hx-w">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>Base58Check encode</b><em>version byte plus payload plus 4-byte checksum</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">Take the input bytes as a payload, prepend a version byte, append the first four bytes of the double SHA-256, and write the lot in base 58. Twenty bytes of payload is what makes a real address.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i" id="hx-i-b58e" rows="1" data-fn="b58e" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" aria-label="Payload for Base58Check encoding"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar">
          <span class="hx-fol" id="hx-fol-b58e">following shared input</span>
          <button type="button" class="hx-re" data-fn="b58e" hidden>follow shared</button>
          <span class="hx-n" id="hx-n-b58e"></span>
          <label class="hx-sel"><span>Version</span>
            <select id="hx-ver" aria-label="Version byte">
              <option value="0">0x00, P2PKH mainnet, 1...</option>
              <option value="5">0x05, P2SH mainnet, 3...</option>
              <option value="111">0x6f, P2PKH testnet, m or n</option>
              <option value="196">0xc4, P2SH testnet, 2...</option>
            </select>
          </label>
        </div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-b58e"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c hx-w">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>Byte order flip</b><em>hex, reversed</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">Reverse the bytes. Transaction ids, block hashes and merkle roots are stored one way and printed the other, which is the single most common reason two tools disagree about the same 32 bytes.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i" id="hx-i-flip" rows="1" data-fn="flip" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" aria-label="Bytes to reverse"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar"><span class="hx-fol" id="hx-fol-flip">following shared input</span><button type="button" class="hx-re" data-fn="flip" hidden>follow shared</button><span class="hx-n" id="hx-n-flip"></span></div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-flip"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c hx-w">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>Base58Check decode</b><em>takes an encoded string, not bytes</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">Paste a legacy address. The checksum is verified against the double SHA-256 of the body, and the version byte is named. Private keys are refused, not decoded.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i hx-i-str" id="hx-i-b58d" rows="1" data-fn="b58d" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" placeholder="1A1zP1eP5QGefi2DMPTfTL5SLmv7DivfNa" aria-label="Base58Check string to decode"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar hx-cbar-ex">
          <div class="hx-ex">
            <span>Try:</span>
            <button type="button" data-b58="1A1zP1eP5QGefi2DMPTfTL5SLmv7DivfNa">genesis address</button>
            <button type="button" data-b58="3P14159f73E4gFr7JterCCQh9QjiTjiZrG">a P2SH address</button>
            <button type="button" data-b58="1A1zP1eP5QGefi2DMPTfTL5SLmv7Divfna">one typo</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-b58d"></div>
      </div>

      <div class="hx-c hx-w">
        <div class="hx-ch"><b>Bech32 and Bech32m decode</b><em>takes an encoded string, not bytes</em></div>
        <p class="hx-cd">Paste a bc1 address. The human-readable part, the checksum constant, the witness version and the witness program all come out, and the version has to match the constant.</p>
        <textarea class="hx-i hx-i-str" id="hx-i-b32d" rows="1" data-fn="b32d" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" autocorrect="off" placeholder="bc1qw508d6qejxtdg4y5r3zarvary0c5xw7kv8f3t4" aria-label="Bech32 string to decode"></textarea>
        <div class="hx-cbar hx-cbar-ex">
          <div class="hx-ex">
            <span>Try:</span>
            <button type="button" data-b32="bc1qw508d6qejxtdg4y5r3zarvary0c5xw7kv8f3t4">P2WPKH</button>
            <button type="button" data-b32="bc1p0xlxvlhemja6c4dqv22uapctqupfhlxm9h8z3k2e72q4k9hcz7vqzk5jj0">P2TR</button>
            <button type="button" data-b32="bc1qw508d6qejxtdg4y5r3zarvary0c5xw7kv8f3t5">bad checksum</button>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="hx-o" id="hx-o-b32d"></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hx-card hx-mt hx-cta-card">
      <div class="hx-lab">Next step</div>
      <p>These are the parts. To watch them assembled into something real, take a signed transaction apart on the <a href="/tools/decode">raw transaction decoder</a>, where the same double SHA-256 produces the txid and the same byte reversal is what turns it into the string block explorers print.</p>
      <a class="hx-cta" href="/tools/decode">Decode a raw transaction</a>
    </div>

    <div class="doc-card hx-mt">
      <div class="doc-sec doc-prose">
        <h2>Why bitcoin hashes twice</h2>
        <p>Almost everywhere bitcoin says hash it means SHA-256 run twice over the same bytes. The habit dates from 2008, when the standard advice for defending against length extension attacks on the SHA-2 family was to hash the digest again, and it stuck: transaction ids, block ids, merkle nodes and the four-byte checksum inside every legacy address are all double SHA-256. The doubling buys little against any attack known today and costs one extra compression per hash, but it is consensus now, so every node computes it the same way. Try the two cards above on the same input and you can see for yourself that the second is not the first hashed by eye, it is a completely unrelated 32 bytes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-sec doc-prose">
        <h2>HASH160, and why addresses are short</h2>
        <p>A public key is 33 bytes compressed, or 65 uncompressed. An address holds only 20, because a key is put through SHA-256 and then RIPEMD-160, a combination bitcoin calls HASH160. Two different hash families in sequence means a break in either one alone still leaves the other standing, and 160 bits is small enough to write down while still needing about 2 to the 80th work to collide. Those 20 bytes are what sits inside a legacy address after the version byte, and what sits inside a native segwit address as the witness program. Load the example public key above, copy the 20 bytes the HASH160 card returns, and paste them into the Base58Check card: out comes an address beginning with 1, which is exactly the sequence a wallet runs when it makes you one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-sec doc-prose">
        <h2>Two checksums, two eras</h2>
        <p>Base58Check, from 2009, drops the four characters that look alike in most fonts (zero, capital O, capital I, lower case l) and protects the rest with four bytes of double SHA-256. It catches essentially any accidental change, but it cannot tell you where the mistake is. Bech32, specified in BIP-173 and amended by BIP-350, replaces that with a BCH error correcting code over 32 characters: it is guaranteed to catch up to four wrong characters, is very unlikely to miss more, and it can point at the character that went wrong. BIP-350 also changed the checksum constant for witness versions 1 and up after a flaw was found in the original, which is why a taproot address is bech32m and a segwit v0 address is not. The <a href="/tools/validate">address validator</a> uses that property to locate single typos.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-sec doc-prose">
        <h2>The byte order trap</h2>
        <p>A transaction id is stored inside blocks and inside other transactions in one byte order, and printed by explorers and wallets in the opposite one. Nothing about the hash changes; only the direction it is written. The genesis merkle root is the classic demonstration: internally it begins 3ba3edfd, and every explorer shows it as 4a5e1e4b, the same 32 bytes read backwards. If a hash you computed looks nothing like the one a tool shows, reverse it before assuming either side is broken. The flip card does exactly that, and it also reads four and eight byte values both ways, which is how transaction amounts, in little endian satoshis, are stored.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="doc-sec doc-prose">
        <h2>A hash is not encryption</h2>
        <p>Every function on this page goes one way. There is no key, nothing is hidden, and nothing can be undone: a digest cannot be turned back into what produced it, which is exactly why it is safe to publish one. That also means hashing a secret does not protect it. If you paste a seed phrase into any web page, hashing it afterwards changes nothing about the fact that the words were typed into a browser, sat in a text field, and may have been captured by an extension, an autofill store or a sync service before a single byte was hashed. Tools that genuinely need a seed belong on an offline machine; <a href="/learn/self-custody">self custody</a> covers where the line sits, and <a href="/learn/scams">common scams</a> covers what happens when people cross it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hx-about doc-prose">
      <p>This page is a workbench, not an authority: it computes digests and checks checksums, and that is all. A valid checksum means the string was typed correctly, never that an address belongs to whoever sent it to you, and a matching hash proves two byte strings are identical, not that either one is honest. To check an address you are about to pay, use the <a href="/tools/validate">address validator</a>; to see these primitives at work inside a real transaction, use the <a href="/tools/decode">raw transaction decoder</a>; for where the double hash came from in the first place, read the <a href="/learn/whitepaper">whitepaper</a>. Everything here is informational under the <a href="/terms">terms</a>; data provenance for the rest of the site is documented at <a href="/sources">/sources</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <aside class="doc-rail">
    <div class="doc-tile">
      <div class="doc-tlab">What runs here</div>
      <div class="hx-t"><b>SHA-256</b><i>From crypto.subtle, the browser's own audited implementation.</i></div>
      <div class="hx-t"><b>RIPEMD-160</b><i>Written out inline: browsers do not expose it. Checked against the specification's own vectors on load.</i></div>
      <div class="hx-t"><b>Base58Check</b><i>Encode and decode, with the four-byte double SHA-256 checksum verified and the version byte named.</i></div>
      <div class="hx-t"><b>Bech32, Bech32m</b><i>BIP-173 and BIP-350 decode: human-readable part, witness version, program, checksum constant.</i></div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-tile">
      <div class="doc-tlab">Checked on load</div>
      <div class="hx-t"><b id="hx-selft">Running self-test</b><i id="hx-selfd">Known answers for every function are recomputed each time this page opens. If one disagrees, the cards refuse to run.</i></div>
    </div>
    <div class="doc-tile">
      <div class="doc-tlab">Related</div>
      <nav>
        <a href="/tools/decode">Raw transaction decoder</a>
        <a href="/tools/validate">Address validator</a>
        <a href="/learn/whitepaper">The whitepaper, explained</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </aside>
  </div>
</main>
<style is:global>
  .hx-card{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:18px 22px 20px}
  .hx-mt{margin-top:14px}
  .hx-top{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:9px}
  .hx-lab{font:700 9.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.13em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3)}
  .hx-modes{display:flex;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap}
  .hx-m{background:var(--panel-2);border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:8px;color:var(--ink-2);
    font:700 10px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:6px 12px;cursor:pointer;white-space:nowrap}
  .hx-m:hover{border-color:rgba(255,166,60,.4);color:var(--ink)}
  .hx-m.is-on{border-color:rgba(255,166,60,.55);background:rgba(247,147,26,.08);color:var(--acc)}
  .hx-ta,.hx-i{width:100%;box-sizing:border-box;display:block;background:rgba(124,140,168,.13);
    border:1px dashed rgba(255,166,60,.32);border-radius:10px;color:var(--price-ink);
    font:600 12.5px/1.6 var(--mono);padding:11px 13px;outline:none;resize:vertical;word-break:break-all}
  .hx-ta{min-height:62px}
  .hx-i{min-height:46px;margin-top:9px}
  .hx-ta:focus,.hx-i:focus{border-style:solid;border-color:rgba(255,166,60,.55)}
  .hx-ta::placeholder,.hx-i::placeholder{color:var(--ph);font-weight:600}
  .hx-ta[disabled],.hx-i[disabled]{opacity:.45;cursor:not-allowed}
  .hx-bar{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:10px}
  .hx-ex{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;flex-wrap:wrap;min-width:0}
  .hx-ex span{font:700 9.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3)}
  .hx-ex button{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:8px;color:var(--ink-2);
    font:700 10.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.08em;padding:6px 11px;cursor:pointer}
  .hx-ex button:hover{background:rgba(36,51,78,.55);color:var(--ink)}
  .hx-cnt{font:600 10px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3);
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;flex:none}
  .hx-fine{font:500 11.5px/1.55 var(--sans);color:var(--ink-3);margin:11px 0 0}
  .hx-priv{font:600 11px/1.6 var(--mono);letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--ink-3);margin:10px 2px 0}
  .hx-seed{margin-top:11px;background:rgba(246,70,93,.09);border:1px solid var(--down);border-radius:10px;
    padding:11px 14px;font:500 12.5px/1.65 var(--sans);color:var(--ink-2)}
  .hx-seed b{color:var(--down);font-weight:700}
  .hx-fail{background:rgba(246,70,93,.09);border:1px solid var(--down);border-radius:12px;padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:14px}
  .hx-fail b{display:block;font:700 14px var(--mono);color:var(--down);letter-spacing:.03em}
  .hx-fail span{display:block;font:500 13px/1.65 var(--sans);color:var(--ink-2);margin-top:6px}

  .hx-cards{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:12px;margin-top:14px}
  .hx-c{background:var(--panel);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:12px;padding:15px 18px 17px;min-width:0}
  .hx-c.hx-w{grid-column:1/-1}
  .hx-ch{display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:9px;flex-wrap:wrap}
  .hx-ch b{font:700 14px var(--sans);color:var(--ink)}
  .hx-ch em{font:600 9px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--acc);font-style:normal}
  .hx-cd{font:500 12px/1.6 var(--sans);color:var(--ink-3);margin:6px 0 0}
  .hx-cbar{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:8px}
  .hx-cbar-ex{margin-top:9px}
  .hx-fol{font:600 9px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3)}
  .hx-fol.is-own{color:var(--acc)}
  .hx-re{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:7px;color:var(--ink-2);
    font:700 9.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:5px 9px;cursor:pointer}
  .hx-re:hover{background:rgba(36,51,78,.55);color:var(--ink)}
  .hx-n{margin-left:auto;font:600 9.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3);
    font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;flex:none}
  .hx-sel{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;min-width:0;flex:1 1 100%}
  .hx-sel span{font:700 9px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3);flex:none}
  .hx-sel select{background:var(--panel-2);border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:8px;color:var(--ink-2);
    font:600 10.5px var(--mono);padding:7px 8px;flex:1 1 auto;min-width:0;max-width:100%;cursor:pointer}

  .hx-o{margin-top:11px}
  .hx-st{font:700 11.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.04em;margin-bottom:8px}
  .hx-st.hx-ok{color:var(--up)}
  .hx-st.hx-bad{color:var(--down)}
  .hx-st.hx-mut{color:var(--ink-3)}
  .hx-r{display:grid;grid-template-columns:104px minmax(0,1fr) auto;gap:10px;align-items:start;
    padding:7px 0;border-bottom:1px dotted var(--line-2)}
  .hx-r:last-child{border-bottom:0;padding-bottom:0}
  .hx-r i{font:700 8.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.11em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--ink-3);
    font-style:normal;padding-top:3px}
  .hx-r u{font:600 12px/1.65 var(--mono);color:var(--ink-2);text-decoration:none;word-break:break-all;display:block;min-width:0}
  .hx-r.hx-key u{color:var(--acc);font-weight:700}
  .hx-r.hx-num u{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums;color:var(--price-ink)}
  .hx-cp{background:transparent;border:1px solid var(--line-2);border-radius:6px;color:var(--ink-3);
    font:700 8.5px var(--mono);letter-spacing:.09em;text-transform:uppercase;padding:4px 7px;cursor:pointer;flex:none}
  .hx-cp:hover{color:var(--acc);border-color:rgba(255,166,60,.5)}
  .hx-note{font:500 12px/1.6 var(--sans);color:var(--ink-3);margin-top:9px}
  .hx-note.hx-warn{color:var(--down)}

